## 8. SMART STUDY PLANNER

Build a planner that helps users allocate time realistically.

Inputs:
- Tasks and their estimated durations.
- Deadlines.
- Importance and urgency.
- Topic difficulty.
- Available study hours.
- Preferred study times.
- Existing calendar commitments when available.
- Revision requirements.
- User-defined daily workload limits.

The AI may propose a schedule, but application code must validate it.

Rules:
- Avoid overlapping sessions.
- Respect availability and deadlines.
- Do not schedule more time than the user has available.
- Reserve reasonable breaks.
- Prioritize urgent and important work.
- Leave buffer time for unexpected delays.
- Never schedule every minute of a day.
- Allow manual overrides.
- Explain scheduling trade-offs.
- Allow users to accept, edit, or reject proposed plans.

If the plan cannot fit all tasks, clearly show the conflict and offer alternatives.

Implement a missed-task recovery flow that helps users reschedule or reduce scope without guilt-inducing language.

## 9. FOCUS TIMER

Implement a reliable focus timer.

Default preset:
- 25 minutes focus.
- 5 minutes short break.
- Optional longer break after several sessions.

Allow custom durations.

### Timer requirements

Support:
- Start.
- Pause.
- Resume.
- Finish early.
- Cancel with confirmation when appropriate.
- Break transitions.
- Session summaries.
- Task association.
- Historical session review.

Store timestamps and calculate elapsed time from them. Do not depend exclusively on setInterval or a continuously running frontend counter.

The timer must recover correctly after:
- Browser refresh.
- App backgrounding.
- Navigation away from the focus screen.
- Temporary device suspension.

Track active time separately from paused time. Count only actual focused intervals toward focused-time analytics.

For mobile, use suitable native lifecycle handling and notifications where permitted.

For web, explain that background execution and notifications have browser limitations.

Never claim that a timer blocks all distractions unless a real, platform-supported blocking feature has been implemented.

